      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Coding Sample: Deficit Tweet Classifier (Python)\"\n",
        "format:\n",
        "  pdf:\n",
        "    number-sections: false\n",
        "    toc: false\n",
        "    highlight-style: pygments\n",
        "fontsize: 11pt\n",
        "geometry: margin=1in\n",
        "execute:\n",
        "  eval: false   # don't execute any code\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Preface\n",
        "\n",
        "**Context.**\n",
        "\n",
        "This script is part of my project *Twitter and the Politics of the Federal Deficit* (\\~3.6M tweets from official congressional accounts, 2017–2023). The goal is to identify deficit-related tweets to build monthly member-level panels used in logistic regressions linking \"deficit\" rhetoric to party control and legislative windows.\n",
        "\n",
        "**What the code does.**\n",
        "\n",
        "(i) Reads raw tweet JSON files (each JSON file is a tweet)\n",
        "\n",
        "\\(ii\\) Flags tweets using a two-tier keyword system (precise phrases OR weaker terms + required context). For instance, a tweet would is labeled as deficit (is_deficit = 1) if it contains an obvious reference to the deficit (i.e, \"national debt\")— or any other phrase in \"strong keywords\" dictionary. Or, it tweet can be classified if it contains the word \"debt\" with a corresponding context word (i.e, \"federal\")\n",
        "\n",
        "\\(iii\\) rites `data/debt_tweet_summary.csv` with `screen_name`, monthly `tweet_date (YYYY-MM)`, and `is_debt` (0/1).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Design choices.** Precision-first tiering— easy to extend / mutate.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Limitations & extensions.**\n",
        "\n",
        "The dictionary approach likely undercounts implicit deficit tweets across all members. It is also difficult to account for rhetorical differences (i.e, how members choose to communicate about the deficit) across parties and individual representatives. For instance, some members may choose to reference the deficit in a more explicit fashion (i.e, \"the federal deficit needs to be reduced\"). Others may choose to discuss the deficit in implicit or vague language, which results in a systematic underrepresentation of their actual deficit tweet rate. I discuss these limitations in more depth in my methodology sections and propose alternative labeling procedures—such as a supervised machine learning approach. However, as I underscore in that section, this approach is not an obvious improvement, as it demands substantially greater time and computational resources while likely offering only marginal gains in accuracy.\n",
        "\n",
        "------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"
      ],
      "id": "6421d840"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Python Code (not executed)\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Tier 1: Strong phrase-based keywords (high precision)\n",
        "\n",
        "strong_keywords = [ \"national debt\", \"national deficit\", \"government debt\", \"government deficit\", \"federal debt\", \"federal deficit\", \"u.s. debt\", \"u.s. deficit\", \"us debt\", \"us deficit\", \"american debt\", \"american deficit\", \"debt ceiling\", \"budget deficit\", \"fiscal responsibility\", \"fiscal cliff\", \"paygo\", \"unsustainable debt\", \"reckless spending\", \"debt crisis\", \"interest on the debt\", \"interest payments\", \"balanced budget\", \"unpaid debt\", \"unpaid tax\", \"trillion-dollar debt\", \"trillion-dollar deficit\", \"burdening future generations\", \"mortgaging our future\", \"out-of-control spending\", \"runaway debt\", \"deficit\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "Tier 2: Weak keywords + context\n",
        "\n",
        "weak_keywords = [\"debt\"] context_words = [ \"spending\", \"federal\", \"budget\", \"tax\", \"government\", \"cut\", \"reduce\", \"increase\", \"decrease\", \"higher\", \"increased\", \"increasing\", \"trillion\", \"reckless\", \"unsustainable\", \"burden\", \"ceiling\", \"cliff\", \"reduction\", \"billion\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "Paths\n",
        "\n",
        "raw_data_path = \"raw_congress_tweets/data\" output_path = \"data/debt_tweet_summary.csv\"\n",
        "\n",
        "records = []\n",
        "\n",
        "for filename in tqdm(sorted(os.listdir(raw_data_path))): if filename.endswith(\".json\"): full_path = os.path.join(raw_data_path, filename) with open(full_path, \"r\") as f: try: tweets = json.load(f) except Exception as e: print(f\"Error reading {filename}: {e}\") continue\n",
        "\n",
        "        for tweet in tweets:\n",
        "            text = tweet.get(\"text\", \"\").lower()\n",
        "            screen_name = tweet.get(\"screen_name\", \"\")\n",
        "            tweet_date = tweet.get(\"time\", \"\")[:7]  # YYYY-MM\n",
        "\n",
        "            # Strong match\n",
        "            is_strong_match = any(kw in text for kw in strong_keywords)\n",
        "\n",
        "            # Weak match: contains debt + context\n",
        "            contains_weak = any(wk in text for wk in weak_keywords)\n",
        "            contains_context = any(ctx in text for ctx in context_words)\n",
        "            is_weak_match = contains_weak and contains_context\n",
        "\n",
        "            is_debt = int(is_strong_match or is_weak_match)\n",
        "\n",
        "            records.append({\n",
        "                \"screen_name\": screen_name,\n",
        "                \"is_debt\": is_debt,\n",
        "                \"tweet_date\": tweet_date\n",
        "            })\n",
        "\n",
        "df = pd.DataFrame(records) df.to_csv(output_path, index=False) print(f\"✅ Saved {len(df)} rows to {output_path}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv(output_path) print(df.head()) print(df[\"is_debt\"].sum())"
      ],
      "id": "fc0c4d69",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    }
